Define `gpio_to_irq()` and `irq_to_gpio()` on `arch/rlx`, which declares both
and defines neither.

WHY THIS EXISTS.  `config/host-compat/0005`'s own closing section names this
defect and hands it on -- "That is `R5-4`'s risk to carry, not this patch's to
fix."  This is `R5-8` discharging it, and one sentence in that hand-off is
refuted below rather than quoted.

THE DEFECT, 量 2026-09-10 over the staged tree of the baseline drop
(`src-vendor/rtl819x-toolchain @ 5c9be5d9`).

  `arch/rlx/include/asm/mach-generic/gpio.h:16-17` declares

      int gpio_to_irq(unsigned gpio);
      int irq_to_gpio(unsigned irq);

  OUTSIDE the `#ifdef CONFIG_GPIOLIB` guard, so both are declared in every
  configuration.  Line 16 is the ONLY occurrence of `gpio_to_irq` anywhere
  under `arch/rlx/` -- one grep, one line, no definition.

  It is not an `arch/rlx` mistake.  量: `arch/rlx/include/asm/mach-generic/
  gpio.h` and `arch/mips/include/asm/mach-generic/gpio.h` are BYTE-IDENTICAL
  in this drop, md5 `8c6bc68ef836ae3f7b36f01b94d37792` both.  The header was
  copied whole and the hole came with it.  `mach-generic` is the fallback for
  a board that supplies its own; 量, no `.c` file under `arch/` in this drop
  defines `gpio_to_irq` in any form, and neither does the vendor's `boards/`
  tree -- so no board here supplies one.

  `include/asm-generic/gpio.h` does not fill it either.  Under `CONFIG_GPIOLIB`
  it declares `__gpio_to_irq` and stops; under `!CONFIG_GPIOLIB` its `#else`
  branch has no `gpio_to_irq` at all.  The arch header is the layer that is
  supposed to connect the two, and rlx's connects three of the four:
  `gpio_get_value`, `gpio_set_value` and `gpio_cansleep` are `#define`d to
  their `__`-prefixed gpiolib bodies on lines 5-7 and `gpio_to_irq` is not.

  `include/linux/gpio.h:98` DOES define a `gpio_to_irq`, and it is unreachable
  here: it sits in the `#else` of `#ifdef CONFIG_GENERIC_GPIO`, and gpiolib
  `select`s `GENERIC_GPIO`, so on any image that has a `gpio_chip` the `#if`
  branch is taken and `<asm/gpio.h>` is what is read.  🔴 A grep for
  "definitions of gpio_to_irq in this tree" returns FIVE and the honest count
  of definitions reachable from `arch/rlx` with gpiolib on is ZERO.  The first
  draft of `R5-8`'s plan said "the whole tree has no definition"; that is the
  weaker true statement and this file states the stronger one instead.

🔴 WHAT 0005 GOT WRONG, AND IT IS THE HALF THAT DECIDES URGENCY.  0005 says
"Nothing in the built set is known to call them".  量: `drivers/gpio/
gpiolib.c:1155` calls `gpio_to_irq()` -- not `__gpio_to_irq()` -- inside
`gpiolib_dbg_show()`, and gpiolib.c IS in the built set on every image since
`r54`.  The call sits inside `#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_FS`, which opens at :1133
and closes at :1263, and this project's images carry
`# CONFIG_DEBUG_FS is not set`, so the preprocessor removes it and the link
succeeds.  `drivers/gpio/rtl819x-gpio.c`'s own file header (NOT ESTABLISHED 3)
recorded that on 2026-09-06 and named this fix.  So the sentence in 0005 is
true only of the CONFIGURATION and reads as a statement about the TREE, which
is the distinction this file exists to keep.

  The consequence, stated as a fact rather than a worry: **on this tree,
  before this patch, `CONFIG_DEBUG_FS=y` and `CONFIG_GPIOLIB=y` together are a
  link failure**, and nothing but a build says so.

THE SECOND CALLER IS THE ONE `R5-8` CAME FOR.  `drivers/input/keyboard/
gpio_keys.c` calls `gpio_to_irq()` at :62, :134, :175, :199, :222 and :240 --
six sites, and :62 is a `BUG_ON` inside the ISR.  Every one is unconditional
within the file.  So `CONFIG_KEYBOARD_GPIO=y` on this tree is a LINK failure
and not a probe failure, which is why `R5-8` writes `rtl819x-keys` at all.

WHERE THE VALUE COMES FROM, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON FOR THE FORM CHOSEN.
讀 `drivers/gpio/gpiolib.c:1097-1103`, verbatim in this drop:

    int __gpio_to_irq(unsigned gpio)
    {
        struct gpio_chip    *chip;

        chip = gpio_to_chip(gpio);
        return chip->to_irq ? chip->to_irq(chip, gpio - chip->base) : -ENXIO;
    }

`drivers/gpio/rtl819x-gpio.c:658` is `.to_irq = NULL`.  So aliasing
`gpio_to_irq` to `__gpio_to_irq` makes `gpio_to_irq(5)` return `-ENXIO`
BECAUSE THIS CHIP HAS NO `to_irq` HOOK -- a value produced by the data
structure, not a constant somebody typed.  The day an `rtl819x-gpio` with a
real `.to_irq` exists, `gpio_to_irq` starts returning a real irq number with
no second patch and no edit here.

WHY NOT x86's SHAPE.  讀 `arch/x86/include/asm/gpio.h:44-47`, inside its
`CONFIG_GPIOLIB` branch:

    /*
     * Not implemented, yet.
     */
    static inline int gpio_to_irq(unsigned int gpio)
    {
        return -ENOSYS;
    }

That is one of the two upstream precedents and it was rejected, not
overlooked.  A constant is a number this project chose, and this project's
rule is that a value entering code needs two agreeing sources; `-ENXIO` from
`chip->to_irq == NULL` needs none, because it is not an input.  It also makes
one of the four gpiolib aliases in this header behave differently from the
other three for no reason a reader could reconstruct.

WHY A `#define` AND NOT A `static inline` WRAPPER.  Two reasons, and the
second is mechanical.  ① Lines 5-7 of this file are already three `#define`s
of exactly this shape; a fourth finishes a list, where an inline would start
a second convention in a twenty-line file.  ② 讀: `__gpio_to_irq` is declared
by `#include <asm-generic/gpio.h>`, which is on line 19 -- BELOW the point
where the declaration being replaced sits.  A `static inline` wrapper written
in place would call an undeclared function; a `#define` has no such ordering
requirement because it is expanded at the call site.

WHY THE DECLARATION MOVES INTO THE `#else` RATHER THAN BEING DELETED.  Under
`!CONFIG_GPIOLIB` this header's `#else` branch declares six GPIO entry points
-- `gpio_request`, `gpio_free`, `gpio_direction_input`,
`gpio_direction_output`, `gpio_get_value`, `gpio_set_value` -- and `arch/rlx`
defines NONE of them, exactly as it defines no `gpio_to_irq`.  That branch is
coherent as it stands: this architecture has no non-gpiolib GPIO
implementation, and any consumer built against it fails at link, loudly, on
whichever symbol it touches first.  Adding a working `gpio_to_irq` there would
fix one of seven and produce a configuration that links a little further
before failing, which is worse than one that fails at the first call.  So the
declaration moves down one line and that branch is left exactly as broken as
it was, deliberately.

`irq_to_gpio` IS DIFFERENT AND IS HANDLED DIFFERENTLY.  There is no
`__irq_to_gpio` in gpiolib -- 量, zero occurrences in the tree -- so no alias
is available and any definition is a constant.  Two upstream sources in this
drop agree on which: `arch/x86/include/asm/gpio.h:49` returns `-EINVAL`, and
`include/linux/gpio.h:105`, the no-GENERIC_GPIO fallback, returns `-EINVAL`
after a `WARN_ON(1)`.  The `WARN_ON` is not copied: it is right for a platform
that has no GPIO support at all and wrong for one that has gpiolib and no GPIO
interrupts, where asking is not a bug.

It is placed AFTER `#include <asm-generic/gpio.h>` and not with the others,
and the position is load-bearing: `EINVAL` reaches this header only through
that include (`asm-generic/gpio.h` opens with `<linux/types.h>` and
`<linux/errno.h>`), and `include/linux/gpio.h` includes `<asm/gpio.h>` on its
line 7 with no `errno.h` before it.  Written in place it would not compile.

⚠️ AND ITS REFUTATION CONDITION IS WEAKER THAN `gpio_to_irq`'s, WHICH IS SAID
HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE FOUND.  量: three files in this drop call
`irq_to_gpio` -- `drivers/power/tosa_battery.c`, `drivers/mmc/host/at91_mci.c`
and `drivers/ata/pata_rb532_cf.c` -- and this board builds none of them.  So
no image this project ships can demonstrate that this half of the patch did
anything.  What demonstrates it is a compile probe run at the desk against the
staged tree, which is the experiment below and not an image.

WHAT THIS CHANGES ON THE BOARD.  `gpio_keys` stops failing at link and starts
failing at probe, with the SoC's own answer in the errno.  讀
`gpio_keys_probe`, in order, for a button on a line this chip knows:

    gpio_request(5, ...)            succeeds -- bit 5 is inside
                                    RTL819X_GPIO_KNOWN_MASK (0x60 in 1.1)
    gpio_direction_input(5)         succeeds
    irq = gpio_to_irq(5)            -ENXIO, from .to_irq == NULL
    if (irq < 0) {
        error = irq;
        gpio_free(button->gpio);    <- THE LINE RELEASES
        goto fail2;
    }
  fail2:
    while (--i >= 0) { ... }        <- with one button i is 0, so the body
                                    does not run: no double free, no leak

So a `gpio-keys` device that fails this way leaves the GPIO line free, and a
driver that requests it afterwards gets it rather than `-EBUSY`.  That is
read out of the source and is NOT the reason `R5-8` keeps `gpio_keys` out of
the image -- the reason is that the order of two probes on one line would rest
on initcall link order, and `rtl819x-keys` carries the same call and prints
its result as a boot mark instead.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT ESTABLISH.  That this SoC has a GPIO interrupt.  It
establishes the opposite, in a form software can read: `gpio_to_irq()` on this
board answers `-ENXIO` and does it by consulting the chip.  `docs/interrupt-
map.md` still has no row for a GPIO interrupt and no GPIO interrupt has been
measured on this die.

REFUTATION CONDITION, WRITTEN BEFORE THE BUILD.

  ① A compile probe -- one translation unit calling both `gpio_to_irq()` and
     `irq_to_gpio()`, compiled with the staged tree's own flags -- must fail
     WITHOUT this patch and succeed WITH it.  Without the patch the failure is
     at link, so the probe is read with `nm`: the unpatched object carries an
     undefined `gpio_to_irq`, the patched one carries an undefined
     `__gpio_to_irq` instead and no `irq_to_gpio` at all.  A probe that passes
     both ways would mean the patch is not the thing being tested.

  ② `CONFIG_KEYBOARD_GPIO=y` must link WITH this patch.  Without it the same
     build must fail naming `gpio_to_irq`.  This is the same claim as ① one
     layer up, on real upstream code rather than on a probe of mine, and it is
     the reason a control image is built at all.

  ③ REFUTED BY any build in which `gpio_to_irq` appears as an undefined symbol
     in the linked `vmlinux`, or in which `CONFIG_DEBUG_FS=y` still fails to
     link on it.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED.  `arch/mips/include/asm/mach-generic/
gpio.h`, the byte-identical copy.  This build is `ARCH=rlx` and no file under
`arch/mips/` is compiled, so patching it would change no artefact and would
double the blast radius of every future rebase of this patch.  The identity is
recorded above because it is evidence about where the defect came from, not
because both copies need fixing.

`HC-1` moves from five declared patches to six.  Which of the six the
directory's name fits, and what is owed about that, is `PROGRESS.md`'s to say.

--- a/arch/rlx/include/asm/mach-generic/gpio.h
+++ b/arch/rlx/include/asm/mach-generic/gpio.h
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 #define gpio_get_value	__gpio_get_value
 #define gpio_set_value	__gpio_set_value
 #define gpio_cansleep	__gpio_cansleep
+#define gpio_to_irq	__gpio_to_irq
 #else
 int gpio_request(unsigned gpio, const char *label);
 void gpio_free(unsigned gpio);
@@ -12,10 +13,23 @@
 int gpio_direction_output(unsigned gpio, int value);
 int gpio_get_value(unsigned gpio);
 void gpio_set_value(unsigned gpio, int value);
+int gpio_to_irq(unsigned gpio);
 #endif
-int gpio_to_irq(unsigned gpio);
-int irq_to_gpio(unsigned irq);
 
 #include <asm-generic/gpio.h>		/* cansleep wrappers */
 
+/* BELOW the include on purpose: EINVAL reaches this header only
+ * through <asm-generic/gpio.h>, and include/linux/gpio.h includes
+ * <asm/gpio.h> with no errno.h ahead of it.  There is no
+ * __irq_to_gpio to alias, so this is a constant, and the two upstream
+ * sources in this drop that answer the question -- arch/x86's
+ * gpio.h:49 and include/linux/gpio.h:105 -- both answer -EINVAL.  The
+ * WARN_ON(1) the second of those carries is deliberately not copied:
+ * on a platform with gpiolib and no GPIO interrupt, asking is not a
+ * bug.  rlxfw R5-8, config/host-compat/0006. */
+static inline int irq_to_gpio(unsigned irq)
+{
+	return -EINVAL;
+}
+
 #endif /* __ASM_MACH_GENERIC_GPIO_H */
